BUILD + RUN (from repo root; gcc is mingw64, present at /c/ProgramData/mingw64):
  gcc -std=c11 -O2 -I runtime/spu -I include/ps3emu \
      runtime/spu/spu_interp.c \
      runtime/spu/spu_channels.c \
      runtime/spu/tests/test_interp_main.c \
      -o test_interp.exe -lm
  ./test_interp.exe    # exit 0 = PASS

CORRECTIONS vs original test field:
- Include <stddef.h> for size_t (the original omitted it; with -std=c11 size_t is otherwise only pulled in transitively and is fragile). 
- The original passed the brnz immediate as a computed C expression `((int)(0x14-0x18))/4 & 0xFFFF`; that evaluates to 0xFFFF but is needlessly obscure — use the literal 0xFFFF (i16=-1).
- The original's "endianness gap" comment on the DMA assert is removed: mfc_do_transfer (spu_dma.h:118-145) is a raw memcpy, NOT a swap, so LS[0x100] bytes are DE AD BE EF and spu_ls_read32 returns 0xDEADBEEF. The assert passes cleanly; there is no gap to dance around when using read32.
- Output exe written to repo-root test_interp.exe (no path that needs creating). Adjust path as desired.

VERIFIED: the encoders' bit positions match spu_disasm.py extraction; the program decodes to the intended mnemonics; the runtime data flow (a, stqd, ai/brnz loop 35x, MFC GET, mailbox, stop) produces r5=42, LS[0x200]=42, LS[0x100]=0xDEADBEEF, mbox=42, status=STOPPED_BY_STOP.
